window.docContents[53].resolve({"/Type-systems/Parametric-polymorphism/#Core-C______--Type-systems--Parametric-polymorphism":{"contents":"A class template is a class with a type parameter, template<typename T> class C, and it is instantiated only in type position, C<int>. The name of an instantiation is the text of its type, Stack<int>, so the name in the source and the name of the expanded class agree. The parser builds it when it reads a type. The grammar admits one type parameter.\n\nInstantiation is substitution, and it precedes every other judgment.The substitution replaces the type T in the base, the fields, the parameters, the results and the bodies, the types of local declarations, of new and of lambdas included. It also substitutes the type arguments in the name of every class it mentions, so Node<T>* inside List<T> becomes Node<int>* in List<int>.The rule applies to a fixed point, because the class it adds may mention another instantiation. After 32 rounds the expansion fails, which rejects a template that mentions ever larger instantiations of itself. The expansion is idempotent, so check and annotate, which runWith calls, both apply it.A template is never checked, only its instantiations are. Two instantiations of one template are two independent classes with no subtype relation between them.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Type systems","header":"7.3. Parametric polymorphism","id":"/Type-systems/Parametric-polymorphism/#Core-C______--Type-systems--Parametric-polymorphism"}});